# Meterless rules

## Vendored engines are read-only

- `engines/` holds the Meterless reference implementations and conformance suites, vendored unchanged from the upstream commit in `engines/UPSTREAM.md`. Never edit, reformat or "fix" a file there.
- Import them as TypeScript modules through tsx, for example `import { HMEM } from "../../engines/hmem/reference/src/index.ts"`.
- Extensions live in `agent/src/` and must keep the engine contract: the H-MEM facade (`agent/src/memory/hmemFacade.ts`, a subclass of `HMEM`) has to pass `engines/hmem/conformance/runner.ts` via `HMEM_IMPL=<absolute path, forward slashes>`.
- Rerun conformance after any change that touches an engine boundary and record the result in `docs/conformance.md`.

## H-MEM

- `new HMEM({ persistDir: "data/hmem", actor: "cw-agent", minerFn })`. `minerFn` calls Nemotron with think off and strict JSON array output; the reference's model-free path is the fallback.
- Every tool result becomes fact records with provenance `{zetaris_object, sql_sha256, endpoint, accn, filed, licence, retrieved_at, latency_ms, cache_state}` and `source: "zetaris:<object>"`. Tags: `utility:<cik10>`, `tag:<xbrl tag>`, `period:<CYyyyyQn>`, `station:<id>`, `state:<XX>`, `sic:<code>`, `filing:<accn>`.
- `recast_comparative` conflicts (the comparative column of the current filing differs from the value first filed for the same period) are queued as `conflict_detected` and resolved by rule R1 (use the comparative as reported in the current filing), with the resolution recorded; both values and both accession numbers stay in the ledger. The analyst can override in the UI (actor `analyst`); the override is logged, nothing is deleted.
- `tag_choice` and `station_choice` facts persist across runs; a warm run skips the probe chunks and counts the skipped chunks.
- Seed at boot from the getting-started `open_data/manifest.json`, its `known_to_fail` issues and this project's own pre-checks (by_station CSVs stale; parquet by_year current). `why(factId)` returns the ledger history for the UI.
- Dream proposals materialise only through the analyst's `approve()`; an approved invariant may only tighten a verdict (lower a confidence label, add a Not-tested item), never loosen it.

## World Model

- `new WorldModel({ storage: "local", namespace: "crosswalk", directory: "data/world", conflictPolicy: "most-recent-wins" })`.
- Entities: utility (`sec:<cik10>`), state, station (`ghcn:<id>`), sic, filing (`sec:<accn>`), quarter. Relationships: `serves` (valid-from/to), `represented_by`, `peer_of`, `reported_in`, `recasts`.
- Provenance `at` comes from the source record (filing `filed` date, GHCN `DATE`), never from retrieval time, so re-ingest is idempotent (empty diff; reference check).
- The vendored `control-plane/live.html` shows only its own seed data; never present it as showing Crosswalk data.

## Markovian

- `new Markovian({ chunkConfig: { chunkSize: 2000, maxChunks: 10, carryoverTokens: 800, overlapTokens: 0 }, historyDir: "data/markovian", generator })`.
- The orchestrator is unchanged; our generator (`agent/src/loop/generator.ts`) runs at most 2 tool calls per chunk and returns marker text: content, one `<NEEDS_TOOL tool=".." input='..'/>` per executed call, `[STATE_CHECKPOINT]` with the typed state JSON, and `[TASK_COMPLETE]` only when the checklist is satisfied.
- Checklist: utility_resolved, tag_chosen, revenue_like_for_like, peers_loaded, weather_target, weather_peers, dq_checked, attribution_done, claim_tested, not_tested_listed, memo_written. The fallback step is the next open checklist item. A run that ends at max-chunks yields a withheld verdict with reason "investigation incomplete".
- Telemetry labels: Ollama `prompt_eval_count`/`eval_count` are "measured"; the naive full-history baseline is "estimated (chars/4)".

## Scout Intent (our partial reimplementation)

- There is no upstream reference. Our subset (`agent/src/contract/`) is labelled "partial reimplementation" everywhere it is described.
- Guard: scan every untrusted text (filing names, SIC descriptions, anything read from a source) before any model sees it; on a hit wrap it in `<untrusted>` and raise the flag `untrusted_text`. Never drop text silently.
- Execution contract: HMAC-SHA256 over canonical JSON with `CW_CONTRACT_SECRET`, policy pack `crosswalk-scout-subset@1`, surfaces `cw-ui` and `cw-agent`, expiry 15 minutes, one-shot use. Reads are low risk; the registrar plans (`register_frames`, `register_concept`, `register_submission`) are medium risk; an analyst override of a recast resolution is high risk and needs an `analyst-approval` with a reason on the `cw-ui` surface. Verified by both the TypeScript side and the Python registrar (`gateway/contracts.py`), which share a test vector. Describe it as an integrity check, not cryptographic-grade security.
